< From September 27 to October 2, 2026, the 14.2 refinement work ran the preregistered studies PREREG16 to PREREG42 (with PREREG28b) against the 14.1 benchmark record. **No engine file, coefficient or default of the shipped 14.1 was changed by any of them, and 14.1 remains the release.** The work produced an adopted scoring convention (volume under C1, or C2 where tags allow), an adopted scorer revision (r2, from PREREG37 on), one regional NS candidate that passes its benchmark gates but has not cleared a Bakuzis grid (v141LTfi), and named failures that locate the remaining NS and QC errors. The QC height anchor track is closed with the shipped 0.111 kept for 14.2: a field tag anchor (1.242) corrects QC height increment on the benchmark and passes a top height gate on measured heights whose 10% limit is still to be confirmed, but it fails the published TOPHT gate and does not clear the Bakuzis QC grid (PREREG38 to PREREG41), and the earlier QC height gates built on row index pairs are corrected by an erratum (PREREG42). Each gated verdict follows a decision rule fixed before any held out value was read, and the one post hoc step, the PREREG21 split of its survivor term, is declared as such in that RESULT.
---
> From September 27 to October 5, 2026, the 14.2 refinement work ran the preregistered studies PREREG16 to PREREG44 (with PREREG28b) against the 14.1 benchmark record. **No engine file, coefficient or default of the shipped 14.1 was changed by any of them, and 14.1 remains the release.** The work produced an adopted scoring convention (volume under C1, or C2 where tags allow), an adopted scorer revision (r2, from PREREG37 on), one regional NS candidate that passes its benchmark gates but has not cleared a Bakuzis grid (v141LTfi), and named failures that locate the remaining NS and QC errors. The QC height anchor track is closed with the shipped 0.111 kept for 14.2: a field tag anchor (1.242) corrects QC height increment on the benchmark and passes a top height gate on measured heights against the 10% FIA measurement quality objective for tree length, but it fails the published TOPHT gate and does not clear the Bakuzis QC grid (PREREG38 to PREREG41), and the earlier QC height gates built on row index pairs are corrected by an erratum (PREREG42). The origin of 0.111 is now traced: no record fits it, and it was set by hand from a July 2026 stand level top height diagnostic (PREREG43). A Bakuzis QC grid started from measured stand lists (PREREG44) does not clear 1.242 either, so neither tested value of the QC multiplier is supported on long run grounds for 14.3. Each gated verdict follows a decision rule fixed before any held out value was read, and the one post hoc step, the PREREG21 split of its survivor term, is declared as such in that RESULT.
49c49
< PREREG38 refit a single anchor on fold A field tags (1.242). On fold B it brings the pooled predicted over observed height increment ratio to 1.053 (0.093 at 0.111), every diameter class within 0.04 m yr⁻¹ of zero and the dominant cohort to 0.994 of measured t2 height (0.898 at 0.111), but the published TOPHT bias is +16.21% against a limit of +14.63%, so it was not adopted. PREREG39 traced the conflict to the observed height rule: 60.6% of observed TOPHT heights come from the generic `himp` curve, which gives 0.892 of measured height, and that step carries 9.29 of the 16.78 point gap between TOPHT and the dominant cohort. PREREG40 rebuilt the top height gate on measured heights against a 10% ceiling taken from the FIA field measurement tolerance for tree length, a value still to be confirmed: the 1.242 arm is +1.60% (-0.05 to 3.25) and 0.111 is -8.45%, and PREREG41 found the reading robust to tree selection on the same trees on both sides (+1.50% and -8.82%), both readings conditional on the unconfirmed limit. The ceiling is loose, since every arm tested passes it.
